# 30. Store Lifecycle UI

Lifecycle controls must use backend commands.

Do not:

```text
PATCH { status: "ACTIVE" }
```

from arbitrary UI forms.

Instead use explicit operations:

```text
POST /admin/stores/:storeId/activate
POST /admin/stores/:storeId/suspend
POST /admin/stores/:storeId/maintenance
POST /admin/stores/:storeId/archive
```

This makes authorization and state-transition validation explicit.

---

# 31. Suspension

Confirmation:

```text
Suspend Store?

Store:
Urban Fashion

Reason:
[________________]

This may prevent new customer transactions.

[Cancel]
[Suspend Store]
```

On success:

```text
StoreSuspended
```

Do not directly cancel orders, modify inventory, or refund payments from this UI.

Each domain reacts according to its own policy.

---

# 32. Maintenance

Maintenance is not the same as suspension.

Maintenance means temporary storefront unavailability while the store remains operationally configured.

Configuration:

```text
Maintenance Message
Start Time
End Time
Allow Admin Access
Allow Staff Access
```

Events:

```text
StoreMaintenanceEnabled
StoreMaintenanceDisabled
```

---

# 33. Error Isolation

Store Details is a composite page.

One failing subsystem should not necessarily break the whole page.

Example:

```text
Store Health

✓ Catalog
✓ Inventory
✓ Shipping
⚠ Payment health unavailable
✓ SEO
```

Display partial failure states.

Do not hide failures silently.

